  <div>
    <h2>My Why</h2>

    <p>
      Memory is the most idiosyncratic, humanistic aspect in us. It is <strong>The Story</strong> that we keep telling ourselves about who we
      are, why we do things the way we do, and how it shapes the person we’re becoming.
      It’s hauntingly beautiful and sometimes painfully poetic, but most
      importantly, it’s <strong>innately prevalent</strong> in our everyday life. 
      <br><br>To me, memory is what gives life <strong
       >Meaning</strong>. How can something so abstract, so difficult to conceptualize, be so
      ingrained in our identity? Clearly, we don’t know much about ourselves as
      we let on.
    </p>

    <p>
      What continues to fascinate me is the question of what makes us <strong>truly us</strong>. 
      <br><br>Not just through intellectualized frameworks, but also through human
      perspectives because <strong>both are connected and part of the same system</strong>. Or as I like to say, we are both the system observer and the system being observed.
    </p>
    <br>
    <p>--</p>
    <p>
      <em>As Jill Bolte Taylor once said, <strong>
        "Most of us think of ourselves as thinking creatures that feel, but
          we are actually feeling creatures that think."</strong></em>
    </p>
    </div>


